1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,966
A human hair is about eighty thousand to a hundred thousand nanometers wide.

2
00:00:05,466 --> 00:00:10,538
The transistors in a new laptop chip are spaced just a few dozen nanometers apart.

3
00:00:10,538 --> 00:00:15,487
More than a thousand of them could line up across the width of that single hair.

4
00:00:15,987 --> 00:00:19,317
So what if you shrank down and walked inside one of those chips?

5
00:00:19,317 --> 00:00:20,774
What would you actually see?

6
00:00:25,033 --> 00:00:25,597
The rules:

7
00:00:25,597 --> 00:00:28,246
you shrink in stages, and nothing can hurt you.

8
00:00:28,246 --> 00:00:32,529
You don't need air, the heat can't burn you, and somehow, you can still see.

9
00:00:33,029 --> 00:00:36,552
Our destination is a laptop chip, Apple's M3 Max.

10
00:00:36,552 --> 00:00:39,427
It holds ninety-two billion transistors.

11
00:00:39,427 --> 00:00:42,806
That's more than ten for every person on Earth.

12
00:00:45,567 --> 00:00:48,864
At one millimeter tall, about the size of a small ant,

13
00:00:48,864 --> 00:00:51,428
the chip looks like a flat, dark building.

14
00:00:51,428 --> 00:00:53,810
That's the package, a protective shell.

15
00:00:54,310 --> 00:00:59,056
The real chip, called the die, is a thin slab of silicon hidden inside,

16
00:00:59,056 --> 00:01:03,402
wired to the outside world through thousands of tiny metal bumps.

17
00:01:03,902 --> 00:01:06,234
That silicon started out as quartz,

18
00:01:06,234 --> 00:01:13,165
and was refined until it was at least ninety-nine point nine nine nine nine nine nine nine percent pure.

19
00:01:13,165 --> 00:01:13,898
Nine nines.

20
00:01:13,898 --> 00:01:17,764
Fewer than one stray atom for every billion silicon atoms.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,837
Shrink a thousand times more, down to one micrometer,

22
00:01:23,837 --> 00:01:25,789
and you land on top of the die.

23
00:01:25,789 --> 00:01:27,237
It's not a smooth roof.

24
00:01:27,237 --> 00:01:31,392
It's a city of copper highways, stacked about fifteen levels deep.

25
00:01:31,892 --> 00:01:34,556
The top levels are wide, to carry power.

26
00:01:34,556 --> 00:01:38,420
As you climb down, the wires get thinner and more crowded.

27
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:43,282
In total, a leading-edge chip can hold tens of kilometers of copper wire.

28
00:01:43,282 --> 00:01:46,479
Some estimates go close to a hundred kilometers.

29
00:01:46,979 --> 00:01:51,717
Every wire is wrapped in insulation, so signals don't leak into their neighbors.

30
00:01:51,717 --> 00:01:55,092
And it all fits into a few square centimeters of silicon.

31
00:01:55,592 --> 00:01:58,109
These highways used to be aluminum.

32
00:01:58,109 --> 00:02:02,064
In 1997, IBM announced chips wired with copper instead.

33
00:02:02,064 --> 00:02:04,868
Copper carries current far more easily,

34
00:02:04,868 --> 00:02:08,392
and nearly every advanced chip has used it since.

35
00:02:09,233 --> 00:02:11,186
Now watch a signal race past.

36
00:02:11,186 --> 00:02:15,763
A modern processor's clock ticks around four billion times a second.

37
00:02:16,263 --> 00:02:21,517
In one of those ticks, even light only travels about seven and a half centimeters.

38
00:02:21,517 --> 00:02:24,401
Signals in these wires are slower than light.

39
00:02:24,401 --> 00:02:29,078
So the whole city has to be planned so that data arrives exactly on time.

40
00:02:31,833 --> 00:02:35,308
Shrink again, until you're just a hundred nanometers tall,

41
00:02:35,308 --> 00:02:37,165
and you reach the ground floor.

42
00:02:37,165 --> 00:02:38,123
The transistors.

43
00:02:38,623 --> 00:02:42,689
They don't look like the flat switches in old textbook diagrams.

44
00:02:42,689 --> 00:02:46,247
Many are built around thin walls of silicon called fins,

45
00:02:46,247 --> 00:02:48,979
standing up like slices of bread in a rack.

46
00:02:48,979 --> 00:02:52,092
A gate drapes over the top and sides of each fin.

47
00:02:52,592 --> 00:02:54,314
Put a voltage on the gate,

48
00:02:54,314 --> 00:02:58,221
and electrons flow along the fin from one end to the other.

49
00:02:58,221 --> 00:02:58,883
Switch on.

50
00:02:58,883 --> 00:03:01,665
Take the voltage away, and the flow stops.

51
00:03:01,665 --> 00:03:02,393
Switch off.

52
00:03:02,393 --> 00:03:03,585
A one, and a zero.

53
00:03:04,085 --> 00:03:08,912
Intel was the first to mass-produce this kind of three-dimensional transistor,

54
00:03:08,912 --> 00:03:09,964
starting in 2011.

55
00:03:10,464 --> 00:03:12,259
The newest designs go further.

56
00:03:12,259 --> 00:03:15,189
They slice the channel into thin stacked ribbons,

57
00:03:15,189 --> 00:03:17,701
and wrap the gate all the way around them.

58
00:03:18,567 --> 00:03:19,971
And look out for dust.

59
00:03:19,971 --> 00:03:20,928
From down here,

60
00:03:20,928 --> 00:03:25,969
a speck of dust one micrometer across would be a boulder ten times your height,

61
00:03:25,969 --> 00:03:29,033
big enough to flatten whole rows of transistors.

62
00:03:29,533 --> 00:03:32,183
That's why chips are made in clean rooms.

63
00:03:32,183 --> 00:03:37,808
Ordinary room air can hold tens of millions of floating particles in every cubic meter.

64
00:03:37,808 --> 00:03:41,946
The cleanest chip-making air is filtered down to only a handful.

65
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:43,931
Here's a surprise.

66
00:03:43,931 --> 00:03:46,382
This is sold as a three-nanometer chip.

67
00:03:46,382 --> 00:03:51,220
So somewhere down here, you'd expect to find something three nanometers wide.

68
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:51,848
You won't.

69
00:03:52,348 --> 00:03:54,310
The name is mostly marketing.

70
00:03:54,310 --> 00:04:01,483
Industry roadmaps put the gates of a so-called three-nanometer process about forty-eight nanometers apart,

71
00:04:01,483 --> 00:04:05,407
and the tightest wires about twenty-four nanometers apart.

72
00:04:05,907 --> 00:04:10,603
The number on the box stopped matching any real part of the transistor years ago.

73
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:15,480
Now shrink to a single nanometer.

74
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:17,913
You're barely bigger than a few atoms.

75
00:04:17,913 --> 00:04:20,986
And the silicon around you isn't smooth anymore.

76
00:04:20,986 --> 00:04:25,277
It's a crystal, with atoms lined up in a perfect repeating pattern,

77
00:04:25,277 --> 00:04:27,646
about a quarter of a nanometer apart.

78
00:04:28,146 --> 00:04:35,658
That's so small that more than three hundred thousand silicon atoms could sit side by side across the width of one human hair.

79
00:04:36,158 --> 00:04:41,643
Under each gate sits a layer of insulation that's supposed to keep electrons out.

80
00:04:41,643 --> 00:04:44,013
By the mid-2000s, in Intel's chips,

81
00:04:44,013 --> 00:04:47,806
that layer had shrunk to about one point two nanometers.

82
00:04:47,806 --> 00:04:49,296
Just five atoms thick.

83
00:04:50,133 --> 00:04:52,984
And at that thickness, something strange happens.

84
00:04:52,984 --> 00:04:55,427
Electrons don't always stay on their side.

85
00:04:55,427 --> 00:04:59,441
Quantum mechanics lets some of them tunnel straight through the wall,

86
00:04:59,441 --> 00:05:01,535
like a ghost walking through a door.

87
00:05:02,035 --> 00:05:04,973
That leak wasted power and heated up the chip.

88
00:05:04,973 --> 00:05:09,059
The fix was a new insulator made with an element called hafnium.

89
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:11,933
A thicker layer that behaves like a thin one.

90
00:05:11,933 --> 00:05:14,806
Intel said it cut the leak more than tenfold.

91
00:05:15,306 --> 00:05:22,382
Intel's cofounder Gordon Moore called it the biggest change in transistor technology since the late 1960s.

92
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:26,184
Now look around.

93
00:05:26,184 --> 00:05:29,991
Billions of switches flipping, billions of times a second.

94
00:05:29,991 --> 00:05:32,813
Every flip turns a little energy into heat.

95
00:05:33,313 --> 00:05:36,023
In the early 2000s, chip makers hit a wall.

96
00:05:36,023 --> 00:05:39,805
Air-cooled processors topped out at roughly a hundred watts.

97
00:05:39,805 --> 00:05:42,830
Clock speeds, which had climbed year after year,

98
00:05:42,830 --> 00:05:47,367
stalled at around four gigahertz, and have stayed near there ever since.

99
00:05:47,867 --> 00:05:51,053
Back in 2001, an Intel executive, Pat Gelsinger,

100
00:05:51,053 --> 00:05:55,897
warned that if chips kept going the way they were, they'd soon be hotter,

101
00:05:55,897 --> 00:05:58,485
for their size, than a nuclear reactor.

102
00:05:58,985 --> 00:06:02,756
So instead of making one core faster, chips got more cores.

103
00:06:02,756 --> 00:06:07,230
The processor in your laptop works like a team, not a single sprinter.

104
00:06:08,067 --> 00:06:10,821
So how do you even build a city this small?

105
00:06:10,821 --> 00:06:11,526
With light.

106
00:06:11,526 --> 00:06:12,999
But not ordinary light.

107
00:06:12,999 --> 00:06:17,675
Extreme ultraviolet, with a wavelength of thirteen point five nanometers.

108
00:06:18,175 --> 00:06:18,871
To make it,

109
00:06:18,871 --> 00:06:23,491
a machine fires tens of thousands of droplets of molten tin every second,

110
00:06:23,491 --> 00:06:25,579
and blasts each one with a laser,

111
00:06:25,579 --> 00:06:29,377
turning it into a plasma hotter than the surface of the Sun.

112
00:06:29,877 --> 00:06:33,282
That light is absorbed by almost everything, even air.

113
00:06:33,282 --> 00:06:37,569
So it travels through a vacuum, bouncing off mirrors so smooth that,

114
00:06:37,569 --> 00:06:40,343
if one were blown up to the size of Germany,

115
00:06:40,343 --> 00:06:44,190
its biggest bump would be about a tenth of a millimeter tall.

116
00:06:45,033 --> 00:06:46,190
But here's the twist.

117
00:06:46,190 --> 00:06:48,392
You could never actually take this trip.

118
00:06:48,892 --> 00:06:50,177
Atoms don't shrink.

119
00:06:50,177 --> 00:06:54,504
At one nanometer tall, you'd be made of just a handful of atoms.

120
00:06:54,504 --> 00:06:57,276
No room for a brain, or eyes, or a heart.

121
00:06:57,776 --> 00:06:59,845
And even with eyes, you'd be blind.

122
00:06:59,845 --> 00:07:04,159
Visible light waves are hundreds of nanometers long, far bigger than you.

123
00:07:04,159 --> 00:07:09,714
Trying to see with them would be like trying to feel a grain of sand while wearing oven mitts.

124
00:07:10,214 --> 00:07:15,247
That's why scientists who study chips up close use electron microscopes instead.

125
00:07:15,247 --> 00:07:17,260
Electrons behave like waves too,

126
00:07:17,260 --> 00:07:21,601
and their waves can be thousands of times shorter than visible light.

127
00:07:22,101 --> 00:07:25,242
That's the same problem chip makers fight every day.

128
00:07:25,242 --> 00:07:28,201
Ordinary light is too big to draw these features,

129
00:07:28,201 --> 00:07:30,435
so they had to invent a shorter kind.

130
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:36,422
For more than fifty years, shrinking kept Gordon Moore's famous prediction alive:

131
00:07:36,422 --> 00:07:40,216
roughly twice as many transistors on a chip every two years.

132
00:07:40,716 --> 00:07:45,881
And now that the city can barely shrink any further, it's starting to grow upward.

133
00:07:45,881 --> 00:07:48,148
Chips are being stacked into towers,

134
00:07:48,148 --> 00:07:51,423
like the high-bandwidth memory inside AI processors,

135
00:07:51,423 --> 00:07:54,509
with layers of memory piled on top of each other.

136
00:07:55,367 --> 00:08:01,495
So inside the chip in your laptop there's a city with more switches than there are people on Earth,

137
00:08:01,495 --> 00:08:03,910
roads only a few dozen nanometers wide,

138
00:08:03,910 --> 00:08:05,953
and walls just a few atoms thick.

139
00:08:06,453 --> 00:08:10,215
If you could shrink down for one hour, which part would you visit first?

140
00:08:10,215 --> 00:08:11,469
Tell me in the comments.
